 ],
 "literal": "Victory-yielding true-sword holding, enemies / battlefield-to-fall conquering, anklet-and-armlet-wearing broad-handed / roaring-sound-of-liquor-having Adiyar chief, / war-slaying wealth-having golden-garland-wearing Anji! / Milk resembling crescent-moon-forehead shining head, / blue sapphire-like-throat-having one-person like, / may you endure, O great lord, you! Ancient-state-having / big-mountain cleft-inside difficult-height-on borne, / small-leaved gooseberry's sweet-fruit, not-thinking-of / (its power of) becoming (immortal), in-your-heart keeping-secret, / death to-be-removed, to-us you-gave!",
 "translation": "O Anji, chief of the Adiyar clan who possess roaring liquor, you who hold the victorious, true sword to conquer enemies so they fall on the battlefield, you of the broad hands adorned with warrior anklets and armlets, wearing a golden garland and possessing war-slaying wealth! Like the One with the blue sapphire throat and the head shining with a crescent forehead resembling milk, may you endure forever, O great lord! Without thinking of its power to grant immortality, keeping that secret within your heart, you gave to me the sweet fruit of the small-leaved gooseberry tree, which grew on a difficult height inside the cleft of a great mountain, so that my death might be averted.",
 "modern": "வெற்றி தரும் கூர்மையான வாளை ஏந்தி, பகைவர்கள் போர்க்களத்தில் வீழுமாறு வென்ற, வீரக்கழலும் வளையலும் அணிந்த பெரிய கைகளைக் கொண்டவனே! ஆரவாரம் மிக்க கள்ளினை உடைய அதியர் குலத் தலைவனே! போரை வெல்லும் செல்வமும் பொன்மாலையும் உடைய அஞ்சியே! பால் போன்ற பிறைநிலவு விளங்கும் நெற்றியை உடைய தலையையும், நீலமணி போன்ற தொண்டையையும் உடைய சிவபெருமானைப் போல, பெருமானே, நீ என்றும் நிலைபெற்று வாழ்வாயாக! பழமையான பெரிய மலையின் பிளவிலே அரிய உயரத்தில் உண்டான, சிறிய இலைகளை உடைய நெல்லி மரத்தின் இனிய கனியை, அதன் தன்மையை (சாகா வரம் தரும் ஆற்றலை) உனக்குள்ளே மறைத்து வைத்துக்கொண்டு, எனக்குச் சாவு நேராதிருக்க அதனை எனக்குக் கொடுத்தாயே!",
 "explanation": "The poet Avvaiyar is praising her patron, the chieftain Adiyaman Neduman Anji, in this famous poem. Adiyaman had obtained a rare, sweet black gooseberry (nelli) fruit from a perilous mountain cleft, which had the power to grant immortality to whoever ate it. Instead of eating it himself to live forever, he selflessly gave it to the poet Avvaiyar so that her wisdom and poetry might endure for the benefit of the world. Avvaiyar compares Adiyaman to Lord Shiva (the blue-throated one who swallowed poison to save the world) to highlight his selflessness. The image of the gooseberry growing on a dangerous, inaccessible mountain cleft emphasizes the difficulty of obtaining it, making his gift even more precious. Key words include 'nelli' (gooseberry), 'sātal nīṅka' (to avert death), and 'mannuka' (may you endure).",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}